{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause: all three recorded hypotheses are wrong. Measured 2026-10-27 21:35–21:55 PDT.\n\n### Observation\n\n1. **The histories merge cleanly.** `git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only tickets origin/tickets` returns **rc 0** and a single tree sha (`442ef07aa67`), with no conflict output. There is no textual conflict between the 4 local-only snapshot commits and the 32 remote commits.\n2. **The local dirty set and the remote-touched set are disjoint.** `git diff --name-only 237b90d origin/tickets` names **30** paths; the tracker worktree reports **468** changed paths; `comm -12` of the two sorted lists is **0**. So the merge would not need to overwrite any locally changed file.\n3. **Every index entry is stat-dirty.** `git diff-files --name-only` in `.tickets-tracker` reports **213,714** paths — the entire index (213,713 entries, 34.7 MB) — in 6 s, stat-only. But `git status --porcelain` reports only ~225–645 genuinely changed paths and takes **49 s**: it must re-hash all 213 k files because the cached stat data does not match this working tree.\n4. **The bound is 30 s.** `sync._GIT_TIMEOUT = 30`; every sync git call goes through `run_git_bounded`, which converts `TimeoutExpired` into a synthetic `CompletedProcess(returncode=124, stderr=\"git timed out after 30s\")`.\n5. The string `git timed out after 30s` was independently observed on this tracker at ~18:40 PDT for a `git commit` (recorded on bug `affordable-omphacite-sunfish`). That is literally `run_git_bounded`'s synthetic message, so this bound demonstrably fires on this tracker.\n\n### Why the index is stat-dirty — the external cause\n\nA concurrent Claude Code session made a **file copy** of the tracker worktree at `.rebar/worktrees/fix-df72-stale-snapshot-close/.rebar/scratch/df72-23558/tracker-copy`. A copied worktree is not a copy of a repository: its `.git` file carries the original's **absolute** pointer verbatim —\n\n```\n$ cat .../tracker-copy/.git\ngitdir: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker\n```\n\n— so `tracker-copy` and the real `.tickets-tracker` are two working trees sharing **one** admin dir, **one** `refs/heads/tickets`, and **one** `index` file. Every git command run in either tree rewrites the shared index's stat cache to describe *that* tree, which invalidates it for the other. Hence the 213,714 stat-dirty entries and the 49 s re-hash.\n\nThat same handle is why this branch diverged at all: the copy's `converge_snapshot_close_metadata_unit` runs under `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off REBAR_SYNC_PULL=off`, so its REBUILD SNAPSHOT commits land on the real `refs/heads/tickets` and are never pushed. It is also the cause of bug `affordable-omphacite-sunfish` — the same shell ran `git -C tracker-copy remote remove` and deleted both remotes from the shared `.git/config`.\n\n### The chain\n\nExternal copied-worktree handle → shared index permanently stat-dirty for `.tickets-tracker` → every index-refreshing git op costs ~49 s → `git merge` (which refreshes the full index before merging) exceeds the 30 s bound → synthetic rc 124 with `\"git timed out after 30s\"` → `merge_recovery.untracked_overwrite_paths` and `local_change_paths` find neither of their markers in that string, so both return `[]` → `_recover_merge_abort` returns `False` → `merge --abort` + `\"tickets sync could not auto-merge origin/tickets — local state kept; run: rebar fsck-recover\"`.\n\n### Verdict on the three hypotheses\n\n1. **Snapshot collision** — REFUTED. `merge-tree` merges the two histories cleanly; there is no snapshot conflict to resolve.\n2. **Merge strategy gap** — REFUTED. No strategy is missing; the tree merge succeeds. Adding a union/ours driver for the snapshot path would have changed nothing, which is exactly why the obvious fix would have been wrong.\n3. **Unrelated local corruption** — CLOSEST, but the snapshot commits are not incidental: they and the wedged merge are two effects of one external cause.\n\n### The rebar-side defect worth fixing\n\nThe divergence is externally caused and is not rebar's bug. **The misreport is.** A wall-clock timeout is rendered as an unresolvable merge conflict, and the operator is sent to `rebar fsck-recover`, which cannot help: nothing is unmergeable, and the next sync will time out identically. This is not specific to this incident — any store large enough, or any host loaded enough, that pushes `git merge` past 30 s produces the same false \"could not auto-merge\". The store is designed to grow, so this is a scaling defect that fails closed with a wrong diagnosis.\n\nProposed remediation, scoped to that:\n\n1. **Classify the timeout outcome distinctly in `_union_merge`.** `run_git_bounded` already marks it unambiguously (rc 124 + its own synthetic string, and it is the sole constructor of that outcome). When the merge fails that way, log a warning that names the timeout, the bound, and the operation — not \"could not auto-merge … run fsck-recover\". Pure logic; adds no mechanism.\n2. **Stop bounding the local index/worktree mutations at the network bound.** `_GIT_TIMEOUT`'s stated purpose in the source is \"Bound git calls (notably the network `fetch`) so a stuck remote can't hang a sync\". That rationale does not transfer to `merge`/`reset --hard`, whose cost is O(index entries) and legitimately exceeds 30 s on a 213 k-entry store. A separate, larger module constant for the local mutations keeps the stuck-remote protection intact without failing healthy work. A plain module constant is not one of the 7 censused mechanism kinds, so the mechanism ratchet is unaffected.\n3. **Do not retry the timeout.** `_with_transient_fault_retry` correctly declines it today (a timeout is not a runner-FS transient); re-running a 49 s op under a 30 s bound would just burn two more bounds. Leave that behavior and pin it with a test.\n\n### Answering the last acceptance criterion\n\n**No, the warning should not become a hard failure for local-CLI reads.** The stale-read exposure is real — the local CLI served reads 32 events behind while warning only on stderr — but sync is best-effort by design and runs implicitly on reads; converting it to a hard failure would break every read for a condition reads can tolerate. The correct fix is the accurate message in (1), which tells the operator what actually happened instead of pointing at a recovery tool that cannot address it.\n\n### Still unverified, and the live blocker\n\n- I have **not** directly captured a `git merge` returning 124 on this tracker. Doing so requires running the merge against the shared tracker, which this ticket forbids. The chain is inferred from: `git merge` performs the same full index refresh that `status` performs; that refresh measures 49 s; the bound is 30 s; and the identical synthetic timeout string was independently observed on this tracker for a commit at 18:40. A regression test will pin the classification directly rather than relying on this host.\n- **The external writer is live and orphaned.** PID 42620, started 21:38:39 PDT, PPID **1**, still running `run_unit.py` against `tracker-copy`; its predecessor PID 30648 ran 17:36–~21:38. Between 21:38 and 21:41 it reset `tickets` back to `465a93c5673^` and committed two fresh REBUILD SNAPSHOTs. **Repairing the main checkout's `tickets` branch is pointless while that process runs** — it re-diverges the branch within minutes. That acceptance criterion is blocked on an operator stopping the orphan; per AGENTS.md a process at PPID 1 is the documented orphan class (`docs/orphaned-processes.md`). Escalating rather than signalling another session's process."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793162825026094630,"uuid":"0361f3c7-4141-433c-97a3-824a4a1d9345"}